<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dresden</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dresden is the capital of the German state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. Residence of the Wettin rulers from the late 15th century, it became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> capital under Augustus the Strong, with buildings such as the Zwinger and the Frauenkirche, and famous art collections. Allied bombing in February 1945 destroyed its centre; the Frauenkirche was rebuilt and reconsecrated in 2005.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dresden.jpg" alt="Dresden" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kolossos · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DD-Schloss-gp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Division of Leipzig of 1485 the brothers Ernest and Albert split the inheritance: Ernest kept the electorate with Wittenberg and most of Thuringia, while Albert received Meissen with <b>Dresden</b> and Leipzig, founding the Albertine line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Railways spread rapidly after the line from Nuremberg to Fürth opened in 1835 and the Leipzig–<b>Dresden</b> line followed in 1839, and the Rhineland, the Ruhr, Saxony and Silesia began to industrialise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1562 the Franciscan friar Diego de Landa burned the Maya books he found at Maní in Yucatán as works of the devil; only four Maya books survive today, in <b>Dresden</b>, Madrid, Paris and Mexico City.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Die Brücke was formed in <b>Dresden</b> in 1905 and Der Blaue Reiter in Munich in 1911, the year Kandinsky painted what he called the first abstract painting and Cubism caused a scandal at the Salon des Indépendants.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>…years after Schneckenburger's death, Karl Wilhelm, music director of Krefeld, set the poem again and performed it with his men's chorus on 11 June, the silver wedding anniversary of Prince Wilhelm of Prussia, the future Emperor Wilhelm I. This setting became popular after it was sung at <b>Dresden</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein</a></li><li><blockquote>At Pillnitz near <b>Dresden</b>, Frederick William II of Prussia and Emperor Leopold II declared their joint support for King Louis XVI against the French Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-27">27 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
